# ─── Everything the pipeline produces ───────────────────────────────────────
# A fresh checkout starts empty. You fill in .env, run the pipeline, and these
# appear. None of them belongs to the repo: analysis.json describes YOUR app,
# and generated-framework/ is yours to maintain by hand from the moment it is
# written. Committing them here would ship one person's OrangeHRM crawl to
# everyone who clones.
analysis.json
framework-draft.md
.framework-draft.lock
generated-framework/

# Importing emit/runtime/support/logger.ts (its own tests, e.g.) creates this at the
# repo root exactly as it does inside a generated project: the log destination path is
# relative to whatever process imports it.
/test-results/

# Raw crawl output. Working material for the explorer, distilled into analysis.json's
# `screens` section — the ladder and boxes it carries are megabytes nothing reads twice.
.crawl/

# The hand-written config. .env.example is the committed template.
.env

docs/
recordings/
prompt-library/
# Subagent-driven-development scratch: ledgers, briefs, review packages
.superpowers/
